{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on R2 revision 13 at exact d0a0ccd4548425393e7d3ef1a7f622af316c21c0, compared with adopted parent 03cb4ee559176508cbd7906a05e843f68b9723cf. This ordinary review covers all three declared design subjects. One P2 remains because R3 revision 13 was explicitly adopted during this review by d7acffc9a7e91b035d6fef31c754014c05046f43. The finding does not backdate that adoption or treat the earlier R3 approval as adoption.\n\n[P2] Reconcile current R2 authority and capacity accounting with adopted R3 revision 13. Main lines 217 and 222–226 still make R3 revision 9 authoritative and call revision 11 a candidate awaiting review. Lines 945–948 postpone the reserved license-refusal rule. Lines 3823–3825 categorically say that a handler-refused request is new work and draws on no reservation. Adopted R3 at 2709006c982b21c2b38db77ec0901606d81e575b, lines 1860–1918, declares that the first decision on a correctly bound numbered license request uses that number's reservation whether applied or refused; it consumes the numbered decision once, and exact duplicates use nothing more. Its lines 2797–2825 also declare the one correctly bound publication withdrawal exception.\n\nThe concrete R3 witness at 1920–1939 shows the consequence: request 1 established the receiving pin, its license elapsed, and the receiving hold ended. The lane has zero free entries but still reserves license requests 2 and 3, export-settled and the closing checkpoint. The first correct request 2 must write refused/target-not-held using its own reserved entry and advancing the highest decided number; T can then record withheld and send export-settled. R2's blanket rule instead classifies the decision as new work and leaves it retrying for capacity. This is a design authority and accounting conflict, not an observed implementation outage.\n\nUpdate current normative references, reservation clauses and related pending/source-owner dispositions to the now-adopted R3 package